# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2 has "heat_vary → heat_mind"; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.2 has "heat_vary"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the same body appears cold to one hand and warm to another" and "heat and cold are affections only of the mind, not patterns of real beings in corporeal substances" are false. Is S2, "Thus, for instance, it is said that heat and cold are affections only of the mind, and not at all patterns of real beings, existing in the corporeal substances which excite them, for that the same body which appears cold to one hand seems warm to another.", true then?
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2 has "figure_vary" and "(heat_vary → heat_mind) → (figure_vary → figure_mind)"; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.3 have "figure_vary" and "figure_vary → figure_mind"; they differ when only "the same body appears cold to one hand and warm to another" and "figure and extension appear various to the same eye at different stations, or to eyes of different texture at the same station" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "the same body appears cold to one hand and warm to another" and "figure and extension appear various to the same eye at different stations, or to eyes of different texture at the same station" are true and "heat and cold are affections only of the mind, not patterns of real beings in corporeal substances" and "figure and extension are not patterns of qualities existing in Matter, nothing settled and determinate without the mind" are false. Is S3, "Now, why may we not as well argue that figure and extension are not patterns or resemblances of qualities existing in Matter, because to the same eye at different stations, or eyes of a different texture at the same station, they appear various, and cannot therefore be the images of anything SETTLED AND DETERMINATE WITHOUT THE MIND?", true then?
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2 has "sweet_vary → sweet_mind"; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.2 has "sweet_vary"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the thing remaining unaltered, its sweetness is changed into bitter, as in a fever or vitiated palate" and "sweetness is not really in the sapid thing" are false. Is S4, "Again, it is proved that SWEETNESS is not really in the sapid thing, because the thing remaining unaltered the sweetness is changed into bitter, as in case of a fever or otherwise vitiated palate.", true then?
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2 has "motion_vary" and "(sweet_vary → sweet_mind) → (motion_vary → motion_mind)"; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2.3 have "motion_vary" and "motion_vary → motion_mind"; they differ when only "the thing remaining unaltered, its sweetness is changed into bitter, as in a fever or vitiated palate" and "if the succession of ideas in the mind becomes swifter, motion appears slower without any alteration in any external object" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "the thing remaining unaltered, its sweetness is changed into bitter, as in a fever or vitiated palate" and "if the succession of ideas in the mind becomes swifter, motion appears slower without any alteration in any external object" are true and "sweetness is not really in the sapid thing" and "motion is not without the mind" are false. Is S5, "Is it not as reasonable to say that MOTION is not without the mind, since if the succession of ideas in the mind become swifter, the motion, it is acknowledged, shall appear slower without any alteration in any external object?", true then?
